;; spec:   §5, §6 (types §6.6, §6.7, §8.2)
;; expect: accept
;; result: 100
;; audit:  clean
;; An in-place update needs a count of one and HAS-WEAK clear (Decided,
;; owner, 2026-09-27): an object that ever had a weak reference is
;; copied on update rather than written in place. The array [0] is
;; held only by the let cell c, and a weak reference was taken of it.
;; array-set! therefore copies: c gets the new array [9], the old one
;; loses its last count and is freed, and @w is nil. Written in place,
;; the weak reference would have seen an immutable object change to
;; [9] and returned 9, which the counting semantics without the
;; in-place optimisation cannot produce (adversary finding 009).
(defun main () -> i64
  (let ((c (cell (array 1 0)))
        (w (weak @c)))
    (do (array-set! &c 0 9)
        (match @w ((some a) (array-get a 0)) (nil 100)))))
